.Dd July 26, 2026
.Dt OPTIQUE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm optique
.Nd fast FreeBSD ports options and dependency configurator for poudriere
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl v | Fl q | Fl m
.Op Fl z Ar set
.Op Fl j Ar jail
.Op Fl p Ar tree
.Op Fl s Op Ar profile
.Op Fl o Ar dir
.Op Fl J Ar jobs
.Op Fl -no-cache
.Op Fl -color Ar when
.Op Cm tui
.Op Fl -drive
.Op Fl f Ar pkglist
.Op Ar origin ...
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm scan
.Op Fl -json
.Op Fl f Ar pkglist
.Op Ar origin ...
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm sync
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl f Ar pkglist
.Op Ar origin ...
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm clean
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl r
.Op Fl u
.Op Fl f Ar pkglist
.Op Ar origin ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reviews and maintains the ports options files that
.Xr poudriere 8
mounts over a build jail's
.Pa /var/db/ports .
It resolves the complete dependency closure of a package list, determines for
every port in it whether its options file is missing, stale, redundant or
inconsistent, and writes all options files in a single atomic pass.
.Pp
It is a replacement for
.Nm poudriere Cm options Fl C Ar list ,
which walks the dependency tree serially and pops one interactive
.Xr dialog4ports 8
window per port, in dependency order, with no overview and no way back.
.Nm
instead presents one full-screen interface over every port in the closure at
once, with the ports that actually need a decision sorted to the top, and
touches nothing on disk until the changes are explicitly applied.
.Pp
Ports are queried by piping a generated wrapper makefile to
.Nm make Fl f Pa /dev/stdin Cm optique-config .
The wrapper includes the port's own
.Pa Makefile
and dumps its options, option groups, descriptions,
.Va IMPLIES
and
.Va PREVENTS
relations,
.Va BROKEN
and
.Va IGNORE
conditions and the unified dependency list as parse-time
.Ic .info
lines, evaluated under the same layered
.Va __MAKE_CONF
that
.Xr poudriere 8
would use.
Queries run in parallel
.Pq up to 16 by default, see Fl J
so a cold closure of about a thousand ports completes in roughly a minute.
Every result is cached
.Pq see Sx FILES
keyed on the ports tree revision, the hash of the merged
.Pa make.conf
layers and the content of the port's own options file, so subsequent runs
re-invoke
.Nm make
only for ports whose inputs actually changed and typically finish in well
under a second.
Renamed ports are resolved through the tree's
.Pa MOVED
file; ports that vanished surface as per-edge errors.
.Ss Global options
These options are accepted before and after the subcommand name.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl z Ar set , Fl -set Ar set
Poudriere set name, used both to resolve the options directory and to layer
.Pa make.conf .
.It Fl j Ar jail , Fl -jail Ar jail
Poudriere jail name.
Used for
.Pa make.conf ,
.Pa blacklist
and options directory resolution.
.It Fl p Ar tree , Fl -ports-tree Ar tree
Poudriere ports tree name.
Defaults to
.Ql default .
The tree's location is read from
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/ports/ Ns Ar tree Ns Pa /mnt ,
falling back to
.Pa /usr/ports .
.It Fl s Oo Ar profile Oc , Fl -synth Oo Ar profile Oc
Configure for
.Xr synth 1
instead of
.Xr poudriere 8 ,
using the named profile; without one, synth.ini's
.Va profile_selected
is honoured, falling back to
.Ql LiveSystem .
See
.Sx SYNTH .
Mutually exclusive with
.Fl z
and
.Fl j .
Because the profile name is optional, a name that could be read as something
else
.Pq a subcommand, an option, or anything containing a slash
must be attached, as
.Ql --synth=name .
.It Fl o Ar dir , Fl -options-dir Ar dir
Use
.Ar dir
as the options directory verbatim, bypassing poudriere resolution entirely.
.It Fl f Ar pkglist , Fl -file Ar pkglist
Read port origins from
.Ar pkglist ,
one
.Ar origin Ns Op @ Ns Ar flavor
per line,
.Ql #
starting a comment
.Pq the Xr poudriere 8 pkglist format .
Repeatable; duplicates across files and command line arguments are dropped.
.It Fl J Ar jobs , Fl -jobs Ar jobs
Number of parallel
.Nm make
queries.
Defaults to
.Ic min Ns Pq 16 , Va ncpu .
.It Fl -no-cache
Neither read nor write the persistent query cache.
.It Fl -color Ar when
When to colorize output:
.Cm auto
.Pq the default ,
.Cm always
or
.Cm never .
Under
.Cm auto
color is emitted only when standard output is a terminal and
.Ev NO_COLOR
is unset;
.Cm always
is an explicit request and overrides
.Ev NO_COLOR .
Currently only the
.Cm scan
status marker column is colorized; the interactive TUI is unaffected.
.It Fl n , Fl -dry-run
Report what
.Cm sync
and
.Cm clean
would change without touching anything.
The TUI ignores this flag: its apply dialog previews every change instead.
.It Fl v , Fl -verbose
More detail per subcommand:
.Cm scan
prints each port's full
.Ql +ON
and
.Ql -OFF
option state plus query warnings,
.Cm sync
prints the final state of every written file, and
.Cm clean
explains why each kept entry is kept, for example which options deviate from
defaults plus
.Pa make.conf .
.It Fl m , Fl -minimal
Keep the options directory minimal on
.Cm sync
and TUI apply: a port whose final configuration equals what defaults plus
.Pa make.conf
already produce gets no options file.
An existing identical file is removed instead of rewritten, so only
deviations are persisted.
Bare defaults also count as decided:
.Cm scan
and the TUI attention views then only flag deviations and conflicts.
.It Fl q , Fl -quiet
Drop the startup banner and the per-port and per-file listings on standard
output.
The summary line and all warnings and errors on standard error remain.
Ignored by
.Cm scan Fl -json ;
mutually exclusive with
.Fl v .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Ss tui
.Nm Cm tui Oo Fl -drive Oc Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
The default subcommand: running
.Nm
with port origins and no subcommand starts the interface.
It requires a real terminal and refuses to start when standard input or output
is not a tty, unless
.Fl -drive
is given
.Pq see Sx DRIVE PROTOCOL .
.Pp
The left pane lists every port in the dependency closure that has options
.Pq optionless ports remain in the graph but are never shown ,
problems first, each with a status marker:
.Bl -tag -width 6n -compact
.It \[u2717]
conflict: the staged options violate
.Va PREVENTS
or a group rule.
.It *
edited: staged changes not yet applied.
.It \&?
unconfigured: no saved options file.
.It \&!
stale: the option list changed since the file was written.
.It \[u2260]
contradicts the
.Pa make.conf
.Va OPTIONS_SET
or
.Va OPTIONS_UNSET
policy; shown only in the
.Ic w
view.
.It \[u2248]
needs no attention because
.Pa make.conf
already decides it; shown only in the
.Ic m
view.
.It \[u2713]
ok.
.El
.Pp
Two further markers may trail the status one:
.Sq \[u2298]
for a port blacklisted for this jail, tree and set, which therefore never needs
attention, and
.Sq \[u26A0]
for a port that is
.Va BROKEN
or
.Va IGNORE Ns d
with its current options.
.Pp
The right pane shows the port's options in framework order under their group
headers, whose kind is enforced on every toggle:
.Dv SINGLE
requires exactly one enabled member,
.Dv RADIO
at most one and
.Dv MULTI
at least one.
.Pp
Each option row carries the staged value, the port default
.Pq Ql def:on No or Ql def:off ,
a
.Ql NEW
badge for options added since the file was written, and a provenance badge:
.Ql mc
for a global
.Va OPTIONS_SET
or
.Va OPTIONS_UNSET ,
.Ql mc:port
for a per-port knob, and
.Ql FORCED
for a
.Va *_FORCE
knob that the options file cannot override, which also locks the toggle.
Options auto-enabled through an
.Va IMPLIES
chain are locked and marked
.Ql implied by X ,
and options carrying
.Va <OPT>_BROKEN
or
.Va <OPT>_IGNORE
get a warning badge.
A yellow option name deviates from the port default; a magenta one
.Pq badge Ql \[u2260]mc
contradicts
.Pa make.conf
policy.
Options that no longer exist are struck through and dropped on apply; options
excluded by the current flavor but managed through the default flavor are
listed in their own section.
.Pp
Toggling an option re-queries the port in the background against a staging
.Va PORT_DBDIR ,
so dependencies that the option adds or removes appear or disappear within a
second or two.
Nothing touches the real options directory until
.Ic a
is pressed, which previews every file as a diff and then writes atomically
.Pq temporary file, Xr fsync 2 , Xr rename 2 .
.Pp
Key bindings:
.Bl -tag -width "Enter, l" -compact
.It Ic j , k , \(ua , \(da
Move the selection;
.Ic g
and
.Ic G
jump to the first and last entry,
.Ic PgUp
and
.Ic PgDn
page.
.It Ic Enter , l
Edit the selected port;
.Ic h
or
.Ic Esc
returns to the list.
.It Ic Space
Toggle the selected option, enforcing group rules.
.It Ic d
Reset the port to its defaults.
.It Ic u
Revert the port to its saved state.
.It Ic U
Undo the last option change, whichever port it was on and whatever made it
.Pq a toggle, Ic d , Ic u , a bulk decision or a restored draft .
Up to 100 changes back; the affected port is re-queried like any other edit.
.It Ic B
Bulk-set one option across all currently visible ports.
The prompt accepts
.Ar OPTION Ns Cm = Ns Cm on Ns | Ns Cm off ,
or the shorthands
.Ar OPTION Ns Cm +
and
.Ar OPTION Ns Cm - .
.It Ic n , p
Jump to the next or previous port needing attention.
.It Ic f
Jump to the next flavor of the same origin.
.It Ic t
Show only ports needing attention, hiding the ok ones.
.It Ic s
Toggle problems-first versus stable alphabetical sort.
Alphabetical order keeps neighbours in place while working down the list;
.Ic n
and
.Ic p
still jump between problems in either order.
.It Ic m
Count stale and unconfigured ports as ok when
.Pa make.conf
already decides every added option
.Pq stale
or every option at all
.Pq unconfigured ;
such ports are marked
.Ql \[u2248] .
.It Ic w
Flag ports whose options contradict the global
.Va OPTIONS_SET
and
.Va OPTIONS_UNSET
policy, marked
.Ql \[u2260] .
.It Ic /
Filter the port list.
.It Ic a
Apply: preview every file diff, then write all files atomically.
.It Ic i
Show details for the selected option: description, group,
.Va IMPLIES
and
.Va PREVENTS ,
and the dependencies enabling it would add.
.It Ic r
Explain why the port is in the closure: its dependency chain and its
dependents.
.It Ic \&? , h , F1
Colour-coded in-interface help listing markers, badges and keys.
.It Ic Ctrl-L
Force a full screen repaint.
.It Ic q , Ctrl-C
Quit.
.El
.Pp
Quitting with staged changes opens a prompt offering to keep them:
.Ic s
saves them as a draft
.Pq see Sx FILES ,
keyed by the options directory,
.Ic d
discards them, and any other key stays in the interface.
The next session on the same options directory restores the draft's staged
edits, dropping options that meanwhile vanished from a port and re-querying the
restored ports so their dependencies reappear.
The draft is cleared as soon as the changes are applied or discarded;
.Ic u
still reverts an individual port.
.Ss DRIVE PROTOCOL
.Nm Cm tui Fl -drive Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
With
.Fl -drive
the interface runs headless, for debugging and end-to-end testing: the closure
is scanned as usual, but instead of taking over a terminal it renders into an
in-memory screen
.Pq 100 by 35 cells by default
and reads one command per line from standard input.
The keymap, the application state and the drawing code are the interactive
ones, so what a dump shows is what a terminal would show.
.Pp
Blank lines and lines starting with
.Ql #
are ignored.
Every other line is acknowledged on standard output with a line starting with
.Ql ok
or
.Ql err ,
except
.Cm dump
and
.Cm state ,
which print their payload instead.
Output is flushed after each command, so the driver can be held open on a pipe.
A malformed command is reported and never ends the session.
Pending background re-queries are merged before each command, exactly as the
interactive event loop does on every tick.
.Bl -tag -width 16n
.It Cm key Ar spec
Press one key.
.Ar spec
is an optional
.Ql ctrl-
and/or
.Ql alt-
prefix followed by either a single character or one of
.Cm enter esc tab backtab space backspace up down left right pgup pgdn home end
.Cm f1 Ns \(en Ns Cm f12 question slash .
Case matters for characters:
.Ql B
is the bulk key,
.Ql b
is not.
.It Cm keys Ar text
Press each character of
.Ar text
in turn, spaces included, for typing into the filter or bulk prompts.
.It Cm dump
Draw a frame and print the screen as
.Ql screen Ar W Ns x Ns Ar H ,
then one line per row with trailing blanks stripped, then
.Ql end .
.It Cm state
Print a one-line JSON object with the fields
.Va focus , selected , visible , listable , refreshing , pending , message ,
.Va overlay , filter
and
.Va dirty .
.Va overlay
names the popup on screen, if any.
.It Cm settle Op Ar timeout_ms
Wait until no background re-query is outstanding
.Pq default 5000 ms ,
answering
.Ql ok settled
or
.Ql err settle timeout .
This is also what lets the 300 ms debounce on option edits expire.
.It Cm resize Ar W Ns x Ns Ar H
Recreate the in-memory screen at that size, clamped to 20\(en500 cells.
.It Cm quit
End the session; so does end of file on standard input, or any key the
interface itself treats as quitting.
.El
.Pp
Nothing is written to the options directory unless the script drives the apply
flow, so pointing
.Fl o
at a scratch directory keeps a session harmless.
.Ss scan
.Nm Cm scan Oo Fl -json Oc Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
Non-interactive status report over the closure, intended as a build gate.
Each port is classified as
.Cm ok ,
.Cm unconfigured
.Pq no saved options file ,
.Cm stale
.Pq the option list changed since the file was written, with the added and removed options named ,
or
.Cm conflict
.Pq the recorded options violate Va PREVENTS No or a group constraint .
Every flagged port also lists the options that
.Pa make.conf
does
.Em not
decide, as
.Ql undecided: ... ,
or is annotated
.Ql [mc-covered \[u2248]]
when
.Pa make.conf
decides all of them.
Ports that are fully
.Cm mc-covered
never demand attention, and neither do blacklisted ports: poudriere will not
build them in this jail, tree and set, so their options cannot hold a build up.
Ports without any options are counted separately and not listed.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -json
Print one machine-readable JSON object on standard output instead of the table;
progress and the summary stay on standard error and
.Fl q
is ignored.
The object has the keys
.Va options_dir ,
.Va ports_tree ,
.Va ports
and
.Va summary .
Each element of
.Va ports
has
.Va port
.Pq Ar origin Ns Oo @ Ns Ar flavor Oc ,
.Va pkgname ,
.Va status
.Pq Ql conflict , Ql unconfigured , Ql stale No or Ql ok ,
.Va undecided ,
.Va added
and
.Va removed
.Pq both for stale ports ,
.Va mc_covered
and
.Va blacklisted .
Non-ok ports come first, then alphabetical order.
.Va summary
holds the counts
.Va total ,
.Va unconfigured ,
.Va stale ,
.Va conflict ,
.Va ok ,
.Va optionless ,
.Va blacklisted
and
.Va attention ,
the last of which drives the exit status.
.El
.Pp
With
.Fl q
the per-port rows and the banner are dropped, leaving only the summary and any
warnings on standard error, which makes
.Nm Fl q Cm scan
usable directly as a
.Xr cron 8
gate that speaks up only when something is pending.
See
.Sx EXIT STATUS .
.Ss sync
.Nm Cm sync Oo Fl n Oc Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
Headless refresh of the options files over the closure: the fast equivalent of
.Nm poudriere Cm options Fl C .
For every port it keeps the saved file's choices for options that still exist,
adopts the effective value
.Pq defaults plus Pa make.conf
for options the file does not know about, and drops options that no longer
exist.
It then reports every file it changed.
A port whose options file already records the same complete option list and the
same enabled set is left untouched; a changed package name alone does not force
a rewrite, matching
.Nm make Cm config-conditional .
.Pp
Options files belonging to ports that lost
.Em all
of their options are removed as dead configuration, but only when no port in
the closure sharing that options file still has options, so a flavor with an
empty option list never deletes the default flavor's file.
Removal deletes the
.Pa options
file and the containing directory, but keeps a user-managed
.Pa options.local
if one is present.
.Pp
All flavors of one origin share a single options file.
It is written from the default flavor's point of view, exactly as
.Nm make config
on the plain origin would; a non-default flavor that disagrees on an option
both flavors carry is reported as a warning rather than silently overwritten.
Disagreements confined to options one flavor does not even have, such as those
removed by
.Va OPTIONS_EXCLUDE ,
are structural and not reported.
.Pp
With
.Fl n
nothing is written and the count of files that would be written and removed is
reported instead.
A later interactive session can revisit anything
.Cm sync
decided.
.Ss clean
.Nm Cm clean Oo Fl n Oc Oo Fl r Oc Oo Fl u Oc Oo Fl f Ar pkglist Oc Op Ar origin ...
.Pp
Garbage-collect the resolved options directory.
By default it walks every entry in the directory and removes those whose port
has vanished from the ports tree, resolving the tree's
.Pa MOVED
file first so that renames and removals are named and explained rather than
guessed.
Plain
.Cm clean
needs no package list, and giving origins or
.Fl f
without
.Fl u
is an error, since it would ignore them.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl r , Fl -redundant
Also remove files whose recorded configuration is exactly what defaults plus
the
.Pa make.conf
overlay produce anyway, so that removing them changes nothing.
.It Fl u , Fl -unused
Also remove entries for ports outside the dependency closure of the given
package list
.Pq positional origins and/or Fl f Ar pkglist ,
which is then required.
If any port in that closure fails to query, the closure is incomplete and
.Nm
refuses to prune anything rather than risk deleting live configuration.
.El
.Sh POUDRIERE INTEGRATION
All poudriere state is read from
.Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d .
If that directory does not exist and neither
.Fl j
nor
.Fl z
was given,
.Nm
falls back to a plain
.Pa /var/db/ports ;
with
.Fl j
or
.Fl z
it is an error.
.Fl o
bypasses this resolution completely.
.Pp
Given
.Fl j Ar jail ,
.Fl p Ar tree
and
.Fl z Ar set ,
the options directory is resolved exactly as
.Xr poudriere 8
resolves it before null-mounting one over the jail's
.Pa /var/db/ports .
The candidates are tried in this order and the
.Em first existing
one wins:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<jail>-<tree>-<set>-options
<jail>-<set>-options
<jail>-<tree>-options
<tree>-<set>-options
<set>-options
<tree>-options
<jail>-options
options
.Ed
.Pp
If none of them exists, applying creates the one
.Nm poudriere Cm options
would have used for the same flags,
.Pa [<jail>-][<tree>-][<set>-]options ,
the tree component only when
.Fl p
was given explicitly.
.Pp
.Pa make.conf
is layered in
.Xr poudriere 8 Ns 's
inclusion order, from least to most specific, with
.Em every
existing file merged, not just the first match:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
make.conf
<set>-make.conf
<tree>-make.conf
<jail>-make.conf
<tree>-<set>-make.conf
<jail>-<tree>-make.conf
<jail>-<set>-make.conf
<jail>-<tree>-<set>-make.conf
.Ed
.Pp
Port queries therefore see exactly what a poudriere build would see, including
.Va DEFAULT_VERSIONS ,
whose entries can change a port's default options, its option list and its
dependencies; a set that overrides for instance
.Ql DEFAULT_VERSIONS+=python=3.12
is honoured throughout the scan, the interface and every written file.
The same holds for the
.Va OPTIONS_SET ,
.Va OPTIONS_UNSET ,
per-port
.Va <PORT>_SET
and
.Va <PORT>_UNSET
and the
.Va *_FORCE
knobs, which is what the
.Ql mc ,
.Ql mc:port
and
.Ql FORCED
badges report.
.Pp
The
.Pa blacklist
chain is read with the same jail, tree and set expansion and, again like
.Xr poudriere 8 ,
every existing file contributes entries:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
blacklist
<set>-blacklist
<tree>-blacklist
<jail>-blacklist
<tree>-<set>-blacklist
<jail>-<tree>-blacklist
<jail>-<set>-blacklist
<jail>-<tree>-<set>-blacklist
.Ed
.Pp
Each line holds one port origin,
.Ql #
starts a comment, and
.Ql *
globs are allowed, as in
.Ql www/nginx* .
Blacklisted ports are marked in
.Cm scan
and in the interface, never demand attention and never trip
.Cm scan Ns 's
exit status gate, whatever their status.
.Ss SYNTH
.Fl s Oo Ar profile Oc
reads the
.Xr synth 1
layout instead, so the same commands serve a synth host:
.Dl optique -s scan -f list
The profile defaults to synth.ini's
.Va profile_selected ,
then
.Ql LiveSystem .
The ports tree is taken from
.Va Directory_portsdir
and the options directory from
.Va Directory_options
in the
.Bq Ar profile
section of
.Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/synth.ini ,
falling back to that file's
.Bq Global Configuration
section and then to
.Pa /usr/ports
and
.Pa /var/db/ports .
.Pa make.conf
is the single file
.Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/ Ns Ar profile Ns Pa -make.conf ,
the one synth appends to each builder's
.Pa make.conf :
there is no layering, and synth has no blacklist.
.Fl o
still overrides the options directory, while
.Fl p
names a poudriere tree and is therefore ignored under
.Fl s ,
which the startup banner notes.
.Pp
When no ports are given at all, synth mode uses the installed packages as
the list, mirroring how synth builds what is installed: the origins come
from
.Ql pkg query -a %o
with flavors from the pkg
.Ql flavor
annotation.
This applies to the TUI,
.Cm scan ,
.Cm sync
and
.Cm clean Fl -unused .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/<jail>-<tree>-<set>-make.conf" -compact
.It Pa ~/.cache/optique/
Persistent query cache, one JSON Lines file per generation, named
.Pa v<schema>-<tree revision>-<make.conf hash>.jsonl .
Overridden by
.Ev XDG_CACHE_HOME ,
in which case
.Pa $XDG_CACHE_HOME/optique/
is used.
Stale generations are pruned automatically.
Disabled entirely by
.Fl -no-cache .
.It Pa ~/.cache/optique/drafts/
Saved staged edits from an interrupted session, one JSON file per options
directory, named after a hash of its path.
.It Pa /tmp/.tmpXXXXXXXX/
Per-run staging directory, honouring
.Ev TMPDIR ,
holding the merged
.Pa make.conf
handed to
.Nm make
and, under
.Pa db/ ,
the staging
.Va PORT_DBDIR
that background re-queries evaluate uncommitted toggles against.
Removed when
.Nm
exits.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/
Poudriere configuration root.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/ports/<tree>/mnt
File holding the path of the named ports tree.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/*options/
Candidate options directories; see
.Sx POUDRIERE INTEGRATION .
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/*make.conf
The merged
.Pa make.conf
layers.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/*blacklist
The merged blacklist chain.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/synth.ini
.Xr synth 1
configuration, read under
.Fl s
for
.Va Directory_portsdir
and
.Va Directory_options .
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/synth/<profile>-make.conf
The profile's
.Pa make.conf
under
.Fl s .
.It Pa /var/db/ports/
Fallback options directory when no poudriere configuration is present.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Cm scan
uses its exit status as a machine-readable gate:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Nothing is pending: every port in the closure is configured and consistent, or
is fully decided by
.Pa make.conf ,
or is blacklisted.
.It 1
A human decision is pending: at least one port is unconfigured or stale with
undecided options, or has a conflict.
.It other
An error occurred, for example an unreadable package list or an unresolvable
ports tree.
.El
.Pp
.Cm tui ,
.Cm sync
and
.Cm clean
exit 0 on success and non-zero on error, with a diagnostic on standard error.
Per-file failures during
.Cm sync
or
.Cm clean
are reported individually and counted in the summary without failing the run.
Invalid command line usage exits 2.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Review the closure of two ports interactively for the poudriere set
.Ql workstation :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique -z workstation www/nginx mail/dovecot
.Ed
.Pp
Do the same from poudriere package lists;
.Fl f
is repeatable and combines with origins on the command line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique -z server -f /usr/local/etc/poudriere.d/pkglist
optique -z server -f base-list -f extra-list www/nginx
.Ed
.Pp
Report which ports are unconfigured or stale, as a table or as JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique -z workstation scan -f pkglist
optique -z workstation scan --json -f pkglist
.Ed
.Pp
Use the exit status as a
.Xr cron 8
gate that only speaks up when a decision is actually pending:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique -z ws -Q scan -f list || mail-me
.Ed
.Pp
Refresh every options file without interaction, first reporting, then writing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique -z workstation sync --dry-run -f pkglist
optique -z workstation sync -f pkglist
.Ed
.Pp
Garbage-collect the options directory, progressively more aggressively:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique -z workstation clean --dry-run
optique -z workstation clean --redundant
optique -z workstation clean --unused -f pkglist
.Ed
.Pp
A full hygiene pass for one package list, refreshing and then
garbage-collecting, suitable for running before a bulk build:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
optique -z ws sync -f list && \e
    optique -z ws clean --unused --redundant -f list
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr make 1 ,
.Xr synth 1 ,
.Xr ports 7 ,
.Xr poudriere 8
